@@ -0,0 +1,224 @@
+# DSolution 1.83" 2-Mic
+
+Bruce port for the **DSolution 1.83 inch 2-Mic** board (ESP32-S3 + NV3023 panel +
+ES7210 mic ADC + ES8311 speaker DAC).
+
+The retail device this board ships in is the **OSTB-2ST-WIFI** cube, which the
+ESPHome/Chinese community calls **"Xingzhi cube 1.83 2mic"**. That name matters: it is
+what makes an independent, working reference for every pin and for the panel
+init/transform available (see *References* at the end).
+
+```
+pio run -e dsolution-183-2mic            # build
+pio run -e dsolution-183-2mic -t upload  # flash (USB-C, native USB-CDC)
+pio device monitor                       # 115200
+```
+
+## Hardware
+
+| Block | Part | Notes |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| MCU | ESP32-S3, 16 MB QSPI flash, 8 MB octal PSRAM @80 MHz | `qio_opi`, `custom_16Mb.csv` |
+| Display | 1.83" IPS, **NV3023** (NewVision) | 240x284 glass, 240x320 controller RAM, 4-wire SPI |
+| Mic ADC | ES7210 @ I2C **0x40** | 2 mics, MIC3 carries the ES8311 echo loopback (AEC reference) |
+| Speaker DAC | ES8311 @ I2C **0x18** | I2S slave, MCLK from the ESP32 |
+| Amplifier | NS4150 class-D | enabled by PA Enable, **GPIO4 = HIGH** |
+| LED | 1x WS2812 | GPIO48, GRB |
+| Buttons | Boot (0), Vol+ (39), Vol- (40) | all active LOW |
+| Power | charge detect 47, battery ADC **17** (1:2 divider, ADC2 ch6) | GPIO38 in the vendor sheet is wrong |
+
+### Pinout
+
+| Function | GPIO | | Function | GPIO |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| LCD CS | 14 | | I2C SDA (codecs) | 12 |
+| LCD DC | 8 | | I2C SCL (codecs) | 11 |
+| LCD RES | 18 | | I2S MCLK | 5 |
+| LCD SCL/CLK | 9 | | I2S BCLK | 15 |
+| LCD SDA/MOSI | 10 | | I2S WS/LRCK | 16 |
+| LCD BL (PWM) | 13 | | I2S DIN (ES7210 -> ESP32) | 7 |
+| LED WS2812 | 48 | | I2S DOUT (ESP32 -> ES8311) | 6 |
+| Button Boot/Sel | 0 | | PA Enable | 4 |
+| Button Vol+ | 39 | | Charge detect | 47 |
+| Button Vol- | 40 | | Battery ADC | 17 |
+| (sleep ctrl) | 21 | | | |
+
+## Display (NV3023)
+
+The NV3023 is **not** an ST7789. It only accepts register writes after the `0xFD`
+unlock pages are selected, and its power/gamma tables are its own; an ST7789 init
+sequence (LovyanGFX `Panel_ST7789`, TFT_eSPI `ST7789_DRIVER`, Arduino_GFX
+`Arduino_ST7789`) leaves the panel black. DisplayDrivers has no NV3023 profile, so
+this board ships its own panel class:
+
+* `NV3023_Panel.h` — `lgfx::Panel_NV3023 : public lgfx::Panel_LCD`, carrying the
+  vendor init table verbatim plus the MADCTL table (rotation -> MV/MX/MY, BGR bit).
+* `include/DisplayDrivers_User_Setup.h` includes it when `DSOLUTION_183_2MIC` is set.
+  That header is the documented DisplayDrivers extension point and the only
+  project-owned file read early enough for `LOVYAN_PANEL` to resolve.
+* The `.ini` selects the backend (`-DUSE_LOVYANGFX=1`, `-DLOVYAN_PANEL=Panel_NV3023`)
+  and describes the panel geometry (`TFT_WIDTH/HEIGHT/MEM_*`, `TFT_ROTATION`,
+  `TFT_INVERTION`, pins, 40 MHz SPI).
+
+Why LovyanGFX and not the Arduino_GFX default: Arduino_GFX builds its panels from a
+fixed factory table, so a board cannot inject a controller it does not know. LovyanGFX
+allows a panel class of its own without patching a library.
+
+Mapping of the vendor's panel table to this port:
+
+| Vendor | Here |
+| --- | --- |
+| Swap XY = true, Mirror X = false, Mirror Y = true | `getMadCtl()` index 3 = `MV|MY` (`0xA0`) |
+| Color order BGR | bit 3 (`0x08`) OR'd in `getMadCtl()` -- independent of `TFT_RGB_ORDER` |
+| Invert colors = true | `-DTFT_INVERTION=1` -> `Panel_LCD::setInvert()` sends `0x21` |
+| Offset X = 36 | derived by Panel_LCD (`memory_height - panel_height`); **not** set as `TFT_OFFSET_*` |
+| Frame 284x240, offset_width 36, offset_height 0 | same window as above: CASET 36..319, RASET 0..239 in landscape |
+| 240x280 panel, 284x240 effective | `TFT_WIDTH=240`, `TFT_HEIGHT=284`, `TFT_MEM_HEIGHT=320` |
+| SPI 40 MHz | `-DTFT_WRITE_FREQ=40000000` -- the rate the working ESPHome config uses; 80 MHz was tried first and is a suspect for the black screen |
+
+Orientation: `-DTFT_ROTATION=3` is the panel's *mounting* offset and `-DROTATION=0`
+the runtime rotation, so the board boots in the vendor landscape (284x240) and
+Config > Rotation cycles through the normal four orientations.
+
+The init table, the MADCTL value (`0xA8` = MV|MY|BGR) and the 36-pixel offset were all
+checked line by line against a working ESPHome config for this exact box
+(`mipi_spi`, `model: CUSTOM`, `data_rate: 40MHz`, `color_order: bgr`,
+`invert_colors: true`, `dimensions 284x240 offset_width 36`,
+`transform swap_xy + mirror_y`) — they match. So if the panel is still dark, the bus is
+the first thing to look at, not the register table.
+
+If the colours come out inverted or the wrong way round on a different production
+batch, the two knobs are `-DTFT_INVERTION` and the `| 0x08` in `getMadCtl()`.
+
+## Audio
+
+Init order follows the vendor's mandatory sequence — **I2C, I2S, ES8311, ES7210, PA
+Enable** — split across the two hooks Bruce calls:
+
+* `_setup_gpio()`: I2C on 12/11 (`sys_i2c`, 100 kHz), I2S pins
+  (`speaker_bus` = bclk 15 / ws 16 / dout 6 / mclk 5, `mic_bus` = bclk 15 / din 7 /
+  ws 16 / `MIC_TYPE_I2S_MSB`), and **PA Enable GPIO4 HIGH immediately**, so the amp is
+  never left floating.
+* `_setup_codec_speaker(true)` (called before playback): ES8311 bring-up at `0x18`
+  (reset, clocks, 16-bit I2S slave, DAC power, 0 dB digital volume), then PA Enable HIGH.
+* `_setup_codec_mic(true)` (called before capture): ES7210 bring-up at `0x40` — the
+  Espressif `esp_codec_dev` register sequence: reset, HPF, analog power, 16-bit I2S
+  slave, clock dividers for MCLK = 256 x fs, MIC1/MIC2 gain 24 dB, MIC3 (AEC
+  reference) 0 dB, then power-on.
+
+MCLK: the ESP32 is the I2S master, so MCLK follows the sample rate at a fixed 256x
+ratio — 6.144 MHz at 24 kHz (vendor table) and 12.288 MHz at 48 kHz, which is the rate
+Bruce's own audio paths run at (speaker playback follows the file, capture is fixed at
+48 kHz). The ES7210 dividers are therefore programmed for the 48 kHz row of the
+Espressif coefficient table; the 256x rule the vendor insists on is unchanged.
+
+The ES7210 needs an MCLK pin, and Bruce's microphone path never routed one. The small
+patch in `src/modules/others/mic.cpp` reuses `bruceConfigPins.speaker_bus.mclk` for the
+mic I2S channel when the board declared one (nothing else drives that pin while the mic
+runs). Without it the mic ADC stays silent.
+
+Volume: `bruceConfig.soundVolume` scales the samples in software; the ES8311 DAC
+volume is left at 0 dB (`0x32 = 0xBF`). The vendor's "42 dB" is the chip's *maximum*
+gain setting, not the default.
+
+ES7210 echo reference: MIC3 is the internal ES8311 -> ES7210 loopback, configured at
+unity gain. Nothing in Bruce consumes it yet (there is no AEC in the firmware), but the
+register setup keeps it clean if an AEC-enabled app is added.
+
+## Buttons
+
+`HAS_3_BUTTONS`: **Prev** = Vol- (40), **Next** = Vol+ (39), **Sel** = Boot (0),
+**Prev+Next** together = Esc. All three are wired to GND (`pullup = true`,
+`activeHigh = false`) and polled through `hal_buttons_poll_3()`.
+
+Note: `src/modules/others/mic.cpp` calls `gpio_reset_pin(GPIO_NUM_0)` when a capture
+session ends (a leftover from boards whose mic power rail sits on GPIO0). Here GPIO0 is
+the Sel button, so `InputHandler()` re-arms the pin (`pinMode(0, INPUT_PULLUP)`) once a
+second -- otherwise Sel would go quiet after the first recording.
+
+## Power / battery
+
+* **Charge detect (47)** — driven by the charger's open-drain status output; pulled up,
+  `isCharging()` returns true while it reads LOW. Flip the comparison in
+  `interface.cpp` if this board's charger is active HIGH.
+* **Battery ADC (17)** — `ANALOG_BAT_PIN=17` with `ANALOG_BAT_MULTIPLIER=2.0f`: a pin
+  dump taken from the stock firmware of this exact box puts the battery divider on
+  GPIO17 = ADC2 channel 6 through a 1:2 divider. The vendor sheet's "Battery Status:
+  GPIO38" cannot be right — the S3 has no ADC channel above GPIO20 — and an earlier
+  revision of this port used it, which is why the gauge read 0 %.
+* **Sleep control (21)** — **not driven any more.** No community source documents a
+  power latch on this board, and driving an unknown pin HIGH at boot is a plausible
+  cause of a boot/reset loop (which a host sees as an intermittent USB connection).
+  Build with `-DDSOLUTION_LATCH_PIN=21` to bring the old behaviour back.
+
+## Files touched outside this folder
+
+Two one-line-ish hooks, both documented in place:
+
+1. `include/DisplayDrivers_User_Setup.h` — includes `NV3023_Panel.h` for this board so
+   `-DLOVYAN_PANEL=Panel_NV3023` resolves.
+2. `src/modules/others/mic.cpp` — routes an MCLK to `MIC_TYPE_I2S_MSB` capture when the
+   board declares one on its speaker bus (needed by the ES7210).
+
+## Status / what is not verified on hardware
+
+Compile status: the environment builds cleanly on CI (`.github/workflows/build-dsolution-183-2mic.yml`
+in the parent workspace repo, which applies the port to upstream Bruce at the pinned commit):
+
+```
+Flash: [===       ]  27.4% (used 4 601 355 bytes from 16 777 216 bytes)
+RAM:   [====      ]  39.8% (used 130 260 bytes from 327 680 bytes)
+firmware.bin          4 601 904 bytes     0 warnings from the board's own files
+firmware-factory.bin  4 667 440 bytes     bootloader+partitions+boot_app0+app, flash at 0x0
+NV3023 init table, MADCTL table and the board's _setup_gpio()/codec hooks are in the image
+```
+
+(CI run 44722383672 on `abd84f4`.)
+
+### First flash on real hardware -- what it showed
+
+The first build was flashed on the real box and came up **black, with a USB connection
+that kept dropping** ("chập chờn"). Two things caused that and both are now fixed here:
+
+1. **Identity.** The panel is an NV3023, and the vendor sheet this port was written from
+   (NV3030B label, battery on GPIO38, sleep latch on GPIO21) does not describe this
+   revision. The init table survived the correction unchanged -- it matches the working
+   ESPHome config for this board -- but the name, the battery pin and the SPI clock did
+   not.
+2. **Flashing the app image at 0x0.** `firmware.bin` is linked for 0x10000; writing it at
+   offset 0 overwrites the bootloader and leaves the ROM loader resetting forever. That
+   looks exactly like "black screen + flaky USB". The build now also emits one merged
+   image, `firmware-factory.bin` (~4.7 MB, not padded), which is the only thing to flash,
+   always at **0x0** -- and it is small enough to send through a browser WebSerial
+   flasher in a couple of minutes:
+
+   ```
+   esptool.py --chip esp32s3 write_flash 0x0 firmware-factory.bin
+   ```
+
+### If it is still dead
+
+Do these in order -- each one produces a different symptom, so they pinpoint the fault:
+
+1. `esptool.py --chip esp32s3 erase_flash`, then write `firmware-factory.bin` at 0x0.
+2. Open the serial monitor at 115200 and reset the board. You should see the ROM banner,
+   then `[board] DSolution 1.83 2-Mic: NV3023 + ES7210/ES8311`. No banner but a repeating
+   ROM banner = the app crashes before `_setup_gpio()` (then it is PSRAM/flash config,
+   not the panel). Banner but no picture = the display bus (try
+   `-DTFT_WRITE_FREQ=20000000`, then check `TFT_BL=13` with a multimeter).
+3. Backlight is GPIO13 and it is *active HIGH* through `hal_bright_attach()`; if the panel
+   is lit but grey/white, the init table ran and only MADCTL/COLMOD are left.
+4. Recovery: the community keeps a **full 16 MB dump of the stock Xiaozhi firmware** for
+   this exact box (`ostb-2st`). Restoring it puts the device back to factory state:
+   `esptool.py --chip esp32s3 write_flash 0x0 xiaozhi_ostb-2st_full16MB.bin`.
+
+## References
+
+* `RealDeco/xiaozhi-esphome` -> `devices/Xingzhi/xingzhi-cube-1.83-2mic.yaml` -- working
+  ESPHome config for this exact board: pins, `psram: octal/80MHz`, `USB_SERIAL_JTAG`
+  logger, and the full `mipi_spi` display block (init sequence, transform, offset, BGR,
+  invert, 40 MHz).
+* `ProD-byte/Maerchenbox` -- pin table "read out of the Xiaozhi firmware" for
+  `ostb-2st-wifi-double-mic`: NV3023 284x240 x-offset 36, battery ADC GPIO17 (ADC2 ch6,
+  1:2), plus the 16 MB recovery image instructions.
+* `espressif/esp_lcd_nv3023` (components.espressif.com) -- Espressif's own NV3023 driver.
diff --git a/boards/dsolution-183-2mic/dsolution-183-2mic.ini b/boards/dsolution-183-2mic/dsolution-183-2mic.ini
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3058069
--- /dev/null
+++ b/boards/dsolution-183-2mic/dsolution-183-2mic.ini
@@ -0,0 +1,143 @@
+; =====================================================================================
+;  DSolution 1.83" 2-Mic  --  ESP32-S3 / NV3023 / ES7210 + ES8311
+;
+;  Hardware
+;    - ESP32-S3, 16 MB QSPI flash, 8 MB octal (OPI) PSRAM at 80 MHz
+;    - 1.83" IPS 284x240, NewVision NV3023 controller, 4-wire SPI  (284x240 landscape)
+;    - ES7210 2-channel mic ADC + ES8311 speaker DAC + NS4150 class-D amplifier
+;    - 1x WS2812 (NeoPixel) on GPIO48
+;    - Buttons: Boot/Sel (GPIO0), Vol+ (GPIO39), Vol- (GPIO40)
+;    - Charge detect GPIO47, battery ADC GPIO17 (1:2 divider)
+;    - Retail name: OSTB-2ST-WIFI ("Xingzhi cube 1.83 2mic") -- see README.md
+;
+;  Build:  pio run -e dsolution-183-2mic
+;  Flash:  pio run -e dsolution-183-2mic -t upload
+;
+;  Read README.md in this folder before flashing: the display needs a panel class of
+;  its own (NV3023_Panel.h) and audio needs the board's codec hooks in interface.cpp.
+; =====================================================================================
+
+[env:dsolution-183-2mic]
+board = esp32s3
+board_build.partitions = custom_16Mb.csv
+board_upload.flash_size = 16MB
+board_upload.maximum_size = 16777216
+; Octal (OPI) PSRAM -> qio_opi. Flash itself stays QIO.
+board_build.arduino.memory_type = qio_opi
+board_upload.speed = 921600
+
+; Compiles this folder's interface.cpp. Without it the weak no-op defaults win and the
+; board boots with no pins, no buttons and no codec initialisation at all.
+build_src_filter = ${env.build_src_filter} +<../boards/dsolution-183-2mic>
+
+; Post-build: merge bootloader + partitions + boot_app0 + app into ONE image
+; (.pio/build/dsolution-183-2mic/firmware-factory.bin) that is flashed at 0x0.
+extra_scripts =
+	${env.extra_scripts}
+	post:boards/dsolution-183-2mic/merge_bin.py
+
+build_flags =
+	${env.build_flags}
+	-Iboards/dsolution-183-2mic
+
+	; ==================== Framework control ====================
+	-DCORE_DEBUG_LEVEL=1
+	-DARDUINO_USB_CDC_ON_BOOT=1   ; Serial over the native USB-CDC port (Serial Commands)
+	-DARDUINO_USB_MODE=1
+	-DBOARD_HAS_PSRAM=1           ; must match board_build.arduino.memory_type above
+	-DDISABLE_ALL_LIBRARY_WARNINGS
+
+	; ==================== Board identity ====================
+	-DDSOLUTION_183_2MIC=1        ; selects the NV3023 panel class in include/DisplayDrivers_User_Setup.h
+	-DDEVICE_NAME='"DSolution 1.83 2Mic"'
+
+	; ==================== Display: NV3023, LovyanGFX backend ====================
+	; LovyanGFX is used (instead of the Arduino_GFX default) because it lets the board
+	; supply a panel class with its own init table -- the NV3023 is not an ST7789 and
+	; must not be driven with the ST7789 table. See NV3023_Panel.h.
+	-DUSE_LOVYANGFX=1
+	-DLOVYAN_BUS=Bus_SPI
+	-DLOVYAN_PANEL=Panel_NV3023
+
+	; Pins (from the vendor pinout)
+	-DTFT_CS=14
+	-DTFT_DC=8
+	-DTFT_RST=18
+	-DTFT_SCLK=9
+	-DTFT_MOSI=10
+	-DTFT_MISO=-1                 ; panel is write-only on this board
+	-DTFT_BL=13                   ; backlight, PWM through hal_bright_*
+
+	; Panel geometry, expressed in the controller's native (untransposed) frame:
+	; 240 columns x 320 rows of RAM, of which the 240x284 glass covers the last 284 rows.
+	;
+	; No -DTFT_OFFSET_* on purpose: the 36-row offset the vendor quotes ("Offset X = 36")
+	; is simply where a 284-pixel window sits inside 320 rows of RAM, and Panel_LCD already
+	; derives it (mw - (pw + ox)). Adding it here as well would shift the image by 36px.
+	-DTFT_WIDTH=240
+	-DTFT_HEIGHT=284
+	-DTFT_MEM_WIDTH=240
+	-DTFT_MEM_HEIGHT=320
+
+	; Mounting. TFT_ROTATION is the panel's mounting offset (not the runtime rotation):
+	; 3 makes the boot orientation index 3 = MADCTL MV|MY|BGR, i.e. the vendor's
+	; "Swap XY, Mirror Y, BGR" landscape. ROTATION is the runtime rotation the user sees
+	; and can change from Config > Rotation; 0 keeps that vendor landscape as the default.
+	-DTFT_ROTATION=3
+	-DROTATION=0
+
+	-DTFT_INVERTION=1             ; vendor: "Invert colors = true" -- mandatory on NV3023
+	-DTFT_IPS=1
+	-DTFT_SPI_HOST=1              ; SPI2_HOST on the S3 (0 is SPI1, unusable for displays)
+	-DTFT_SPI_MODE=0
+	-DTFT_WRITE_FREQ=40000000     ; 40 MHz: the only rate a working config uses for this
+	                              ; panel (ESPHome mipi_spi, data_rate 40MHz). 80 MHz was
+	                              ; tried and is a prime suspect for the black screen.
+	-DTFT_READ_FREQ=16000000
+	-DTFT_BUS_SHARED=false        ; nothing else on the display SPI bus
+
+	; ==================== Audio (ES7210 mic ADC + ES8311 DAC + NS4150) ====================
+	; Pins and the codec register programming live in interface.cpp:
+	;   _setup_codec_speaker()  -> ES8311 @0x18, then PA Enable (GPIO4) HIGH
+	;   _setup_codec_mic()      -> ES7210 @0x40
+	; I2C (SDA 12 / SCL 11) is registered as sys_i2c in _setup_gpio().
+	-DHAS_SPEAKER=1
+	-DHAS_MICROPHONE=1
+
+	; ==================== LED ====================
+	-DHAS_RGB_LED=1
+	-DRGB_LED=48
+	-DLED_TYPE=WS2812B
+	-DLED_ORDER=GRB
+	-DLED_COUNT=1
+	-DLED_COLOR_STEP=5
+
+	; ==================== Battery ====================
+	; Battery is on GPIO17 = ADC2 channel 6 through a 1:2 divider (confirmed against a
+	; pin dump taken from the stock firmware of this exact box). The vendor sheet's
+	; "Battery Status: GPIO38" is wrong for this revision -- GPIO38 has no ADC channel
+	; on the ESP32-S3 -- see README.md.
+	-DANALOG_BAT_PIN=17
+	-DANALOG_BAT_MULTIPLIER=2.0f
+	-DANALOG_BAT_MIN_MV=3000
+	-DANALOG_BAT_MAX_MV=4200
+
+	; ==================== Buttons ====================
+	; 3 buttons: btn1 = Vol- = Prev, btn2 = Vol+ = Next, btn3 = Boot = Sel.
+	; Prev+Next together = Esc.
+	-DHAS_3_BUTTONS=1
+
+	; ==================== Power / sleep ====================
+	; Boot (GPIO0) is both the Sel button and the deep-sleep wake source, so the shared
+	; goToDeepSleep()/power-save path has something to wake the board with.
+	-DDEEPSLEEP_WAKEUP_PIN=0
+	-DDEEPSLEEP_PIN_ACT=0
+
+	; ==================== USB ====================
+	-DUSB_as_HID=1
+
+lib_deps =
+	${env.lib_deps}
+	; The S3 + Arduino 3.3.x combination needs LovyanGFX from develop: the 1.2.7 release
+	; predates Arduino 3.3.4 (see lib/DisplayDrivers/README.md, "Installing").
+	https://github.com/lovyan03/LovyanGFX#develop
diff --git a/boards/dsolution-183-2mic/interface.cpp b/boards/dsolution-183-2mic/interface.cpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..408673a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/boards/dsolution-183-2mic/interface.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,349 @@
+// =====================================================================================
+//  boards/dsolution-183-2mic/interface.cpp
+//
+//  DSolution 1.83" 2-Mic -- ESP32-S3, NV3023 240x284 IPS, ES7210 mic ADC + ES8311
+//  speaker DAC + NS4150 amplifier, WS2812 on GPIO48.
+//
+//  What this file does, in boot order:
+//    _setup_gpio()        runtime pin defaults (I2C / I2S / IR / RF), PA Enable HIGH,
+//                         sleep control HIGH, charge detect input, button HAL
+//    _post_setup_gpio()   backlight PWM (needs the TFT to be initialised first)
+//    InputHandler()       3-button poll (Vol- = Prev, Vol+ = Next, Boot = Sel)
+//    _setup_codec_speaker()   ES8311 register bring-up (0x18) -> PA Enable HIGH
+//    _setup_codec_mic()       ES7210 register bring-up (0x40)
+//    isCharging() / powerOff()
+//
+//  The display is configured entirely by the .ini (LovyanGFX panel class
+//  NV3023_Panel.h); nothing display-related is needed here.
+// =====================================================================================
+
+#include "core/bus_HAL.h"
+#include "core/powerSave.h"
+#include "hal/bright/bright.h"
+#include "hal/device.h"
+#include "hal/inputs/buttons.h"
+#include <Wire.h>
+#include <esp_sleep.h>
+#include <globals.h>
+#include <interface.h>
+
+// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+//  Wiring (vendor pinout)
+// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+#define PIN_I2C_SDA 12 // codec control bus (ES7210 + ES8311)
+#define PIN_I2C_SCL 11
+
+#define PIN_I2S_MCLK 5  // 6.144 MHz at 24 kHz; 12.288 MHz at 48 kHz (ratio 256)
+#define PIN_I2S_BCLK 15
+#define PIN_I2S_WS 16
+#define PIN_I2S_DIN 7  // ES7210 -> ESP32 (mic data)
+#define PIN_I2S_DOUT 6 // ESP32 -> ES8311 (speaker data)
+#define PIN_PA_ENABLE 4 // NS4150 enable: HIGH = speaker on (mandatory)
+
+#define PIN_BTN_PREV 40 // Volume down
+#define PIN_BTN_NEXT 39 // Volume up
+#define PIN_BTN_SEL 0   // Boot
+
+#define PIN_CHARGE_DETECT 47 // charger status in (active LOW on most charger ICs)
+#define PIN_BAT_ADC 17       // battery divider -> ADC2 channel 6 (1:2), *not* GPIO38
+#define PIN_SLEEP_CTRL 21    // from the vendor sheet; not driven by default (see _setup_gpio)
+
+// Codec I2C addresses (7-bit)
+#define ES8311_ADDR 0x18
+#define ES7210_ADDR 0x40
+
+// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+//  Buttons -- HAS_3_BUTTONS in the .ini: btn1 = Prev, btn2 = Next, btn3 = Sel,
+//  Prev+Next together = Esc. All three are wired to GND, so active LOW + pull-up.
+// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+static DeviceButtons buttonsCfg() {
+    DeviceButtons cfg;
+    cfg.btn1 = PIN_BTN_PREV;
+    cfg.btn2 = PIN_BTN_NEXT;
+    cfg.btn3 = PIN_BTN_SEL;
+    cfg.pullup = true;
+    cfg.activeHigh = false;
+    return cfg;
+}
+
+// =====================================================================================
+//  Codec helpers
+// =====================================================================================
+
+static void codecWrite(uint8_t addr, uint8_t reg, uint8_t value) {
+    TwoWire *wire = getSysI2CBus();
+    if (!wire) return;
+    wire->beginTransmission(addr);
+    wire->write(reg);
+    wire->write(value);
+    wire->endTransmission();
+}
+
+static inline void es8311Write(uint8_t reg, uint8_t value) { codecWrite(ES8311_ADDR, reg, value); }
+static inline void es7210Write(uint8_t reg, uint8_t value) { codecWrite(ES7210_ADDR, reg, value); }
+
+// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+//  ES8311 (speaker DAC)
+//
+//  I2S slave, 16-bit, MCLK taken from the MCLK pin with a 256 x fs ratio (vendor:
+//  6.144 MHz at 24 kHz). The register values mirror the ES8311 bring-up already used by
+//  boards/ES3C28P, which runs this chip at that same 256 x fs ratio.
+// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+static void es8311Init() {
+    es8311Write(0x00, 0x1F); // reset
+    delay(20);
+    es8311Write(0x00, 0x00); // release reset
+    es8311Write(0x00, 0x80); // power on
+
+    es8311Write(0x01, 0x3F); // all clocks on, MCLK from the MCLK pin
+    es8311Write(0x02, 0x00); // pre_div = 1, pre_multi = 1x  -> 256 x fs
+    es8311Write(0x03, 0x10); // ADC OSR
+    es8311Write(0x04, 0x10); // DAC OSR
+    es8311Write(0x05, 0x00); // ADC/DAC clock divider = 1
+    es8311Write(0x06, 0x03); // BCLK divider (master mode only)
+    es8311Write(0x07, 0x00); // LRCK divider high
+    es8311Write(0x08, 0xFF); // LRCK divider low
+
+    es8311Write(0x09, 0x0C); // SDP in : 16-bit I2S
+    es8311Write(0x0A, 0x0C); // SDP out: 16-bit I2S
+
+    es8311Write(0x0D, 0x01); // power up analog
+    es8311Write(0x0E, 0x02); // analog PGA / modulator
+    es8311Write(0x12, 0x00); // power up DAC
+    es8311Write(0x13, 0x10); // enable HP drive output
+    es8311Write(0x1C, 0x6A); // ADC EQ bypass / DC offset cancel
+    es8311Write(0x37, 0x08); // bypass DAC equaliser
+
+    es8311Write(0x32, 0xBF); // DAC digital volume: 0 dB (software volume scales this)
+}
+
+// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+//  ES7210 (2-mic ADC, with the ES8311 output looped back on MIC3 as the AEC reference)
+//
+//  Sequence and clock coefficients are the ones from Espressif's esp_codec_dev driver:
+//  software reset, high-pass filters, analog power, I2S format, sample-rate dividers
+//  for MCLK = 256 x fs, mic gains, then power-on.
+//
+//  Bruce's microphone path runs at 48 kHz, so MCLK is 12.288 MHz (coefficient
+//  {12288000, 48000, adc_div 1, dll on, doubler on, osr 0x20, lrck 0x01/0x00}).
+//  Same 256 x fs rule as the vendor's 24 kHz / 6.144 MHz pair.
+// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+static void es7210Init() {
+    // --- reset + high-pass filters ---
+    es7210Write(0x00, 0xFF); // software reset
+    es7210Write(0x00, 0x32);
+    es7210Write(0x01, 0x3F); // ADC clocks off while configuring
+    es7210Write(0x09, 0x30);
+    es7210Write(0x0A, 0x30);
+    es7210Write(0x23, 0x2A); // HPF, ADC1/2
+    es7210Write(0x22, 0x0A);
+    es7210Write(0x20, 0x0A); // HPF, ADC3/4
+    es7210Write(0x21, 0x2A);
+
+    // --- slave I2S (bit0 = 0) ---
+    es7210Write(0x08, 0x00);
+
+    // --- analog power + mic bias ---
+    es7210Write(0x40, 0xC3);
+    es7210Write(0x41, 0x70); // MIC1/2 bias
+    es7210Write(0x42, 0x70); // MIC3/4 bias
+
+    // --- data format: 16-bit, MIC1/2 on SDOUT1 ---
+    es7210Write(0x11, 0x60);
+    es7210Write(0x12, 0x00);
+
+    // --- clock dividers for MCLK = 12.288 MHz / fs = 48 kHz ---
+    es7210Write(0x02, 0x01 | 0x40 | 0x80); // adc_div 1, doubler on, dll on  -> 0xC1
+    es7210Write(0x07, 0x20);               // OSR
+    es7210Write(0x04, 0x01);               // LRCK divider high
+    es7210Write(0x05, 0x00);               // LRCK divider low
+
+    // --- mic power + enable ---
+    es7210Write(0x47, 0x08);
+    es7210Write(0x48, 0x08);
+    es7210Write(0x49, 0x08);
+    es7210Write(0x4A, 0x08);
+    es7210Write(0x06, 0x04); // power down DLL (as the Espressif driver does)
+    es7210Write(0x4B, 0x0F); // MIC1/2 bias + ADC + PGA on
+    es7210Write(0x4C, 0x0F); // MIC3/4 bias + ADC + PGA on
+    es7210Write(0x00, 0x71);
+    es7210Write(0x00, 0x41); // device enabled
+
+    // --- gains: MIC1/MIC2 = 24 dB (reg value 8), MIC3 (AEC reference) = 0 dB ---
+    // Bit 4 of each gain register selects the differential input pair.
+    es7210Write(0x43, 0x10 | 8); // MIC1
+    es7210Write(0x44, 0x10 | 8); // MIC2
+    es7210Write(0x45, 0x10 | 0); // MIC3 -- ES8311 echo reference, unity gain
+    es7210Write(0x46, 0x10 | 0); // MIC4 -- unused
+}
+
+// =====================================================================================
+//  Codec hooks called by the audio modules (weak defaults in src/modules/others/*.cpp)
+// =====================================================================================
+
+/*********************************************************************
+** Function: _setup_codec_speaker
+** Enables / disables the speaker path.
+**
+** The order here is the vendor's mandatory one: the codec is configured over I2C
+** first, and the amplifier (PA Enable, GPIO4) is only driven HIGH afterwards. GPIO4 is
+** also set HIGH early in _setup_gpio() so the amp is never left floating.
+**********************************************************************/
+void _setup_codec_speaker(bool enable) {
+    if (enable) {
+        es8311Init();
+        delay(10);
+        digitalWrite(PIN_PA_ENABLE, HIGH); // NS4150: HIGH = amplified output on
+    } else {
+        digitalWrite(PIN_PA_ENABLE, LOW);
+        es8311Write(0x0D, 0xFC); // power down the analog block
+        es8311Write(0x00, 0x00); // hold the codec in reset
+    }
+}
+
+/*********************************************************************
+** Function: _setup_codec_mic
+** Enables / disables the ES7210 microphone ADC.
+**********************************************************************/
+void _setup_codec_mic(bool enable) {
+    if (enable) {
+        es7210Init();
+    } else {
+        es7210Write(0x00, 0x00); // reset / power down
+        es7210Write(0x4B, 0x00);
+        es7210Write(0x4C, 0x00);
+    }
+}
+
+// =====================================================================================
+//  Setup
+// =====================================================================================
+
+/***************************************************************************************
+** Function name: _setup_gpio()
+** Description:   first stage -- pins, buses, input HAL. Runs before the display,
+**                the filesystem and brucePins.conf.
+***************************************************************************************/
+void _setup_gpio() {
+    Serial.begin(115200);
+    Serial.println("[board] DSolution 1.83 2-Mic: NV3023 + ES7210/ES8311");
+
+    // ---- I2C: codec control bus (100 kHz, the vendor's recommended speed) ----
+    bruceConfigPins.sys_i2c = {(gpio_num_t)PIN_I2C_SDA, (gpio_num_t)PIN_I2C_SCL};
+    setSysI2CBus(&Wire);
+
+    // No second, user-facing I2C header on this board: the only exposed pins are the
+    // two volume buttons. Pointing i2c_bus at the codec bus keeps the Config > I2C
+    // menus usable (shared sys/i2c pins are handled by bus_HAL).
+    bruceConfigPins.i2c_bus = {(gpio_num_t)PIN_I2C_SDA, (gpio_num_t)PIN_I2C_SCL};
+
+    // ---- Audio ----
+    // Speaker: {bclk, ws/LRCK, dout, mclk}. MCLK comes from the ESP32 (the ES8311 is an
+    // I2S slave) at 256 x fs.
+    bruceConfigPins.speaker_bus = {
+        (gpio_num_t)PIN_I2S_BCLK, (gpio_num_t)PIN_I2S_WS, (gpio_num_t)PIN_I2S_DOUT, (gpio_num_t)PIN_I2S_MCLK
+    };
+    // Microphone: {clk/bclk, data/din, ws, type}. The ES7210 is an I2S ADC feeding DIN.
+    bruceConfigPins.mic_bus = {
+        (gpio_num_t)PIN_I2S_BCLK, (gpio_num_t)PIN_I2S_DIN, (gpio_num_t)PIN_I2S_WS, MIC_TYPE_I2S_MSB
+    };
+
+    // ---- Amplifier enable: HIGH immediately after boot (vendor requirement) ----
+    pinMode(PIN_PA_ENABLE, OUTPUT);
+    digitalWrite(PIN_PA_ENABLE, HIGH);
+
+    // ---- Power / sleep control ----
+    // Deliberately NOT driven. No community source documents a power latch on this
+    // board (the pin dump from the stock firmware has no GPIO21 at all), and driving an
+    // unknown pin HIGH at boot is a plausible cause of the boot/reset loop that shows up
+    // as an intermittent USB connection. Build with -DDSOLUTION_LATCH_PIN=21 to test it.
+#ifdef DSOLUTION_LATCH_PIN
+    pinMode(DSOLUTION_LATCH_PIN, OUTPUT);
+    digitalWrite(DSOLUTION_LATCH_PIN, HIGH);
+#endif
+
+    // ---- Charge detect + battery status ----
+    pinMode(PIN_CHARGE_DETECT, INPUT_PULLUP);
+    pinMode(PIN_BAT_ADC, INPUT);
+
+    // ---- Buttons ----
+    hal_buttons_init(buttonsCfg(), 3);
+
+    // ---- No expansion header on this board: leave IR/RF/UART unset ----
+    bruceConfigPins.irTx = GPIO_NUM_NC;
+    bruceConfigPins.irRx = GPIO_NUM_NC;
+    bruceConfigPins.rfTx = GPIO_NUM_NC;
+    bruceConfigPins.rfRx = GPIO_NUM_NC;
+}
+
+/***************************************************************************************
+** Function name: _pre_storage_gpio()
+** Description:   runs after the first TFT use, before LittleFS/SD are mounted.
+***************************************************************************************/
+void _pre_storage_gpio() {}
+
+/***************************************************************************************
+** Function name: _post_setup_gpio()
+** Description:   display + storage are up: backlight and anything sharing their buses.
+***************************************************************************************/
+void _post_setup_gpio() {
+    // Backlight on GPIO13, PWM through the shared brightness HAL. main.cpp applies the
+    // user's saved brightness right after this returns; 100% here only covers boot.
+    hal_bright_attach(TFT_BL);
+    hal_bright_set(TFT_BL, 100);
+}
+
+/*********************************************************************
+** Function: _setBrightness
+** Applies a 0-100 brightness value (settings / screen-dim).
+**********************************************************************/
+void _setBrightness(uint8_t brightval) { hal_bright_set(TFT_BL, brightval); }
+
+/*********************************************************************
+** Function: InputHandler
+** Runs in taskInputHandler every ~10 ms. The task clears the input globals and holds
+** the input lock around this call, so it only has to poll the button HAL.
+**********************************************************************/
+void InputHandler(void) {
+    // deinitMicroPhone() in src/modules/others/mic.cpp calls gpio_reset_pin(GPIO_NUM_0) when
+    // a capture session ends (a leftover from boards whose mic power rail sits there). On
+    // this board GPIO0 is the Sel button, so re-arm it instead of losing the button after
+    // the first recording. pinMode() on an input is cheap and idempotent.
+    static uint32_t lastArm = 0;
+    if (millis() - lastArm > 1000) {
+        lastArm = millis();
+        pinMode(PIN_BTN_SEL, INPUT_PULLUP);
+    }
+    hal_buttons_poll_3(buttonsCfg());
+}
+
+// =====================================================================================
+//  Power
+// =====================================================================================
+
+/*********************************************************************
+** Function: isCharging
+** Charge detect on GPIO47. Charger ICs drive their status pin open-drain, so the pin is
+** pulled up and reads LOW while charging. If this board's charger works the other way
+** round, flip the comparison below.
+**********************************************************************/
+bool isCharging() { return digitalRead(PIN_CHARGE_DETECT) == LOW; }
+
+/*********************************************************************
+** Function: powerOff
+** Mutes the amplifier, kills the backlight and goes to deep sleep with the Boot button
+** (GPIO0) as the wake-up source. The power latch (if this board has one) is only released
+** when the build defines DSOLUTION_LATCH_PIN.
+**********************************************************************/
+void powerOff() {
+    hal_bright_set(TFT_BL, 0);
+    digitalWrite(PIN_PA_ENABLE, LOW);
+    es8311Write(0x0D, 0xFC);
+#ifdef DSOLUTION_LATCH_PIN
+    digitalWrite(DSOLUTION_LATCH_PIN, LOW); // release the power latch (board powers down)
+#endif
+    delay(50);
+
+    esp_sleep_enable_ext0_wakeup((gpio_num_t)PIN_BTN_SEL, 0); // wake on Boot = LOW
+    esp_deep_sleep_start();
+}
diff --git a/boards/dsolution-183-2mic/merge_bin.py b/boards/dsolution-183-2mic/merge_bin.py
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..372b2f9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/boards/dsolution-183-2mic/merge_bin.py
@@ -0,0 +1,133 @@
+# =====================================================================================
+#  boards/dsolution-183-2mic/merge_bin.py
+#
+#  Post-build hook: merges bootloader + partition table + boot_app0 + application into
+#  ONE image that is flashed at 0x0, so the board only ever needs a single .bin:
+#
+#      .pio/build/dsolution-183-2mic/firmware-factory.bin
+#
+#      esptool.py --chip esp32s3 write_flash 0x0 firmware-factory.bin
+#
+#  The image is *not* padded to the flash size: it ends right after the application
+#  (~4.7 MB instead of 16 MB), because the whole point of this file is to be flashed
+#  through a browser over WebSerial, and a third of the transfer time is a third of the
+#  chances to drop the link on a board whose USB is already flaky. Erase the chip first
+#  (one click in esptool-js, or `esptool.py erase_flash`) and the stale partition table /
+#  filesystem are gone anyway. Build with DSOLUTION_PAD_FACTORY=1 in the environment to
+#  get the padded 16 MB variant back.
+#
+#  The merge itself is done here, in pure Python, instead of shelling out to
+#  `esptool merge_bin`. esptool renamed that subcommand *and* its --flash_* options
+#  between v4 and v5, which is what made the first CI run of this hook fail with a bare
+#  "unrecognized arguments"; laying the images down at their offsets is exactly what
+#  merge_bin does internally anyway, and PlatformIO has already written the flash
+#  parameters into the bootloader image header.
+#
+#  PlatformIO's own upload target still works (`pio run -t upload` writes the four
+#  separate images); this is only the "one file" output.
+#
+#  Registered from the board .ini:
+#      extra_scripts = ${env.extra_scripts}
+#          post:boards/dsolution-183-2mic/merge_bin.py
+# =====================================================================================
+
+Import("env")
+
+from pathlib import Path
+
+TAG = "[merge_bin]"
+
+# ESP32-S3 boot loader / application images start with the 0xE9 magic byte.
+IMAGE_MAGIC = 0xE9
+
+FLASH_SIZES = {
+    "1MB": 1 << 20,
+    "2MB": 2 << 20,
+    "4MB": 4 << 20,
+    "8MB": 8 << 20,
+    "16MB": 16 << 20,
+    "32MB": 32 << 20,
+}
+
+
+def _flash_size_bytes(value, default="16MB"):
+    key = str(value or default).strip().upper().replace(" ", "")
+    return FLASH_SIZES.get(key, FLASH_SIZES[default])
+
+
+def _pad_wanted():
+    """DSOLUTION_PAD_FACTORY=1 in the shell (or in project options) -> 16 MB image."""
+    import os
+    return str(os.environ.get("DSOLUTION_PAD_FACTORY", "")).strip() not in ("", "0", "no", "false")
+
+
+def merge_bin(source, target, env):
+    build = Path(env.subst("$BUILD_DIR"))
+    app = build / f"{env.subst('$PROGNAME')}.bin"
+
+    bootloader = build / "bootloader.bin"
+    partitions = build / "partitions.bin"
+    boot_app0 = build / "boot_app0.bin"
+    if not boot_app0.is_file():
+        # Framework layout differences: the file lives somewhere under the packages dir.
+        boot_app0 = next(Path(env.subst("$PROJECT_PACKAGES_DIR")).rglob("boot_app0.bin"), boot_app0)
+
+    segments = [(0x0, bootloader), (0x8000, partitions)]
+    if boot_app0.is_file():
+        segments.append((0xE000, boot_app0))
+    else:
+        print(f"{TAG} boot_app0.bin not found -- otadata stays erased, factory app still boots")
+    segments.append((0x10000, app))
+
+    for _, image in segments:
+        if not image.is_file():
+            raise SystemExit(f"{TAG} missing segment: {image}")
+
+    flash_size = _flash_size_bytes(env.BoardConfig().get("upload.flash_size", "16MB"))
+
+    image = bytearray()
+    placed = []
+    for offset, path in segments:  # already ascending
+        data = path.read_bytes()
+        if not data:
+            raise SystemExit(f"{TAG} empty segment: {path}")
+        if len(image) > offset:
+            raise SystemExit(
+                f"{TAG} {path.name} at 0x{offset:X} overlaps the previous segment "
+                f"(which already reaches 0x{len(image):X})"
+            )
+        image.extend(b"\xff" * (offset - len(image)))
+        image.extend(data)
+        placed.append((path.name, offset, len(data)))
+
+    if len(image) > flash_size:
+        raise SystemExit(f"{TAG} merged image {len(image)} B does not fit in {flash_size} B")
+    if _pad_wanted():
+        image.extend(b"\xff" * (flash_size - len(image)))
+        size_note = f"padded to {flash_size} B"
+    else:
+        size_note = f"{len(image)} B, not padded (DSOLUTION_PAD_FACTORY=1 for the 16 MB variant)"
+
+    # Sanity: every image we placed must still be byte-identical where we put it and the
+    # bootloader/app must carry the ESP image magic. A silent offset mistake here is a
+    # bricked-looking board, so fail the build instead of shipping it.
+    for offset, path in segments:
+        data = path.read_bytes()
+        if image[offset:offset + len(data)] != data:
+            raise SystemExit(f"{TAG} verification failed for {path.name} at 0x{offset:X}")
+    for offset, path in ((0x0, bootloader), (0x10000, app)):
+        if image[offset] != IMAGE_MAGIC:
+            raise SystemExit(
+                f"{TAG} {path.name} at 0x{offset:X} does not start with 0x{IMAGE_MAGIC:02X} "
+                f"(got 0x{image[offset]:02X}) -- wrong file at that offset?"
+            )
+
+    out = build / f"{env.subst('$PROGNAME')}-factory.bin"
+    out.write_bytes(image)
+
+    for name, offset, length in placed:
+        print(f"{TAG}   0x{offset:06X}  {name:<18} {length:>9} B")
+    print(f"{TAG} OK: {out.name} {len(image)} B ({size_note}) -- flash it at 0x0")
+
+
+env.AddPostAction("$BUILD_DIR/${PROGNAME}.bin", merge_bin)
